falls  so i recommend that everybody who tests  positive for covid  be monitoring their oxygen levels and be  in touch with their doctor if they see  that number fall  below 93 percent but you know the cdc  website i know you know this doesn't  specifically recommend these oximeters  at home and even some doctors  who when they heard that you were coming  on say they don't think this is a good  idea  they believe it's more accurate to  measure a patient's breath per minute  to determine the oxygen levels what is  your response to that  well you know the best hospitals in the  country uh  are doing this so you know mayo clinic  are doing what uh  occasional or doing what we're doing are  doing pulse  pulse oximetry monitoring of covet  positive patients  so uh you know these oximeters that you  buy at the pharmacy  are reliable uh and you should be  following them and trending them and be  in touch with your doctor but what i'm  telling you is that  the best hospitals in the country are  doing this uh the state of  vermont is only one of 50 states that's  doing it  but of note new york city in the last  two weeks  has now distributed hundreds of  thousands of oximeters  to hospitals so they can monitor every  covet positive patient in new york city  britain nhs england just distributed 200  000 oximeters  so we're seeing a growing movement of  governmental agencies and i hope this  happens in the u.s across  states of monitoring covet positive  patients with oximeters because earlier  detection of illness leads to earlier  treatment  and much better outcomes as we saw with  president trump  and boris johnson i do think what you're  saying  you know makes sense the earlier you  treat it the better the earlier detected  but because  the cdc doesn't recommend it i think  people should check with their doctors  i've heard you say that doctors are  being set up to fail with covet 19. what  do you mean by that  well sadly for my icu colleagues when  you get  patients who have diffuse lung disease  what we call ards  where every area of lung is involved  where oxygen absorption is really  already impaired  where patients lungs have become so  stiff that they can't breathe  those are patients who wind up on  ventilators and and this disease  unfortunately when you get to this  advanced  lung injury we do very poorly with it  there is still  unacceptable high rates of death among  people who get  intubated and hospitalized now it's  important to note that we have reduced  the death rate  uh to one quarter of what it was in new  york city  you know simple things we learned early  on in the pandemic in new york have  really changed practice  that using dexamethasone which is an  inexpensive widely available drug  simple things turning people onto their  stomachs using nasal oxygen instead of  ventilators  we've made tremendous progress but you  do not want to get to the hospital with  very low oxygen and severe lung injury  and so that's what i'm hoping your  listeners hear  all right dr richard leverson thank you  very much
 

